## 1. What is Dependency Injection (DI)?

| Concept | Short Description |
|---------|--------------------|
| **Inversion of Control (IoC)** | The control of creating and managing objects is *inverted* from the application code to a container (e.g., Spring). |
| **Dependency Injection (DI)** | A specific IoC pattern where an object’s *dependencies* (other objects it needs to work) are *injected* (provided) by the container instead of the object creating them itself. |

Why is it useful?

| Benefit | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------|
| **Decoupling** | The class no longer knows how to create its dependencies – it just declares “I need a `PaymentService`.” |
| **Testability** | In tests you can inject mocks or stubs instead of the real implementation. |
| **Configurability** | You can swap implementations (e.g., switch from `EmailServiceImpl` to `SmsServiceImpl`) without touching business code. |
| **Lifecycle Management** | Spring can manage singleton vs prototype scopes, initialization/cleanup hooks, etc. |

## 2. Spring’s DI Mechanism

Spring uses a container called **ApplicationContext** (or `BeanFactory`) to:

1. **Scan / Read** configuration (Java `@Configuration` classes, XML, or component‑scan annotations).
2. **Create** bean definitions.
3. **Instantiate** beans (using constructors, setters, or reflection).
4. **Inject** dependencies into beans (constructor, setter, or field injection).

> **Tip:** Constructor injection is the safest and most recommended approach (especially for mandatory dependencies).

## 3. A Minimal Working Example

We’ll build a tiny “order processing” app:

```
OrderService  --> uses OrderRepository
OrderRepository --> uses DataSource
```

### 3.1. Maven (or Gradle) Dependencies

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependencies>
    <!-- Spring Context -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-context</artifactId>
        <version>5.3.32</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Optional: Spring JDBC (for DataSource) -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-jdbc</artifactId>
        <version>5.3.32</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- For a simple in‑memory H2 database -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.h2database</groupId>
        <artifactId>h2</artifactId>
        <scope>runtime</scope>
        <version>2.2.224</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

> For a plain Java demo you can just add the Spring JARs manually.

### 3.2. Domain / Interface

```java
// OrderRepository.java
public interface OrderRepository {
    void saveOrder(String order);
}
```

### 3.3. Implementation of Repository

```java
// JdbcOrderRepository.java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;

@Repository  // Marks it as a Spring bean
public class JdbcOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    // Constructor injection
    public JdbcOrderRepository(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    @Override
    public void saveOrder(String order) {
        try (Connection con = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO orders (description) VALUES (?)")) {

            ps.setString(1, order);
            ps.executeUpdate();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```

### 3.4. Service Using the Repository

```java
// OrderService.java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service  // Marks it as a Spring bean
public class OrderService {

    private final OrderRepository repository;

    public OrderService(OrderRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public void placeOrder(String order) {
        // Business logic could go here
        repository.saveOrder(order);
    }
}
```

### 3.5. Configuration – Declaring the DataSource

```java
// AppConfig.java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.ComponentScan;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.jdbc.datasource.DriverManagerDataSource;

@Configuration
@ComponentScan("com.example")   // Adjust the package name
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public DriverManagerDataSource dataSource() {
        DriverManagerDataSource ds = new DriverManagerDataSource();
        ds.setDriverClassName("org.h2.Driver");
        ds.setUrl("jdbc:h2:mem:testdb;DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1");
        ds.setUsername("sa");
        ds.setPassword("");
        return ds;
    }
}
```

> The `@ComponentScan` tells Spring to look for `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, etc. in the specified package and register them as beans.

### 3.6. Running the Application

```java
// Main.java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.AnnotationConfigApplicationContext;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bootstrapping Spring
        AnnotationConfigApplicationContext ctx = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

        // Retrieve bean
        OrderService orderService = ctx.getBean(OrderService.class);

        // Use it
        orderService.placeOrder("New Laptop");

        ctx.close();
    }
}
```

When `OrderService` is requested, Spring:

1. Instantiates `JdbcOrderRepository` (needs a `DataSource`).
2. Instantiates `DriverManagerDataSource` (defined in `AppConfig`).
3. Passes the `DataSource` into `JdbcOrderRepository`’s constructor.
4. Passes the `JdbcOrderRepository` into `OrderService`’s constructor.

All wiring happens automatically – no `new` in `Main`.

### 3.7. Alternative Injection Methods

| Injection Type | Code | When to use |
|----------------|------|-------------|
| **Constructor** | `public OrderService(OrderRepository repo)` | Preferred for mandatory dependencies. |
| **Setter** | `public void setRepository(OrderRepository repo)` | Useful for optional dependencies or when you need to rewire after construction. |
| **Field** | `@Autowired private OrderRepository repo;` | Quick but makes unit testing harder (needs reflection or a test framework that supports it). |

---

## 4. Quick Summary

| Step | What Spring Does |
|------|------------------|
| 1. Define beans (`@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Configuration`‑`@Bean`) | Declares *what* objects exist. |
| 2. Scan & register beans | Spring builds a *registry* of bean definitions. |
| 3. Resolve dependencies | For each bean, Spring looks at its constructor/setters/fields, finds matching beans, and injects them. |
| 4. Manage lifecycle | Call `@PostConstruct` / `@PreDestroy` hooks, manage scopes, etc. |

### Why is DI with Spring **better** than manual `new`?

| Manual `new` | With Spring DI |
|--------------|----------------|
| Tight coupling (class knows about concrete implementation). | Loose coupling (class only knows about interface). |
| Harder to test (you must instantiate real objects). | Easy to test (inject mocks). |
| Harder to switch configurations (hard‑coded URLs, credentials). | Externalize config (properties, XML, profiles). |
| Manual lifecycle management (closing connections). | Container handles it. |

---

## 5. A Quick Unit‑Test Example

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.mockito.Mockito;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

class OrderServiceTest {

    @Test
    void shouldSaveOrder() {
        OrderRepository repo = mock(OrderRepository.class);
        OrderService service = new OrderService(repo);

        service.placeOrder("Book");

        verify(repo).saveOrder("Book");
    }
}
```

Notice how we *didn’t* involve Spring at all. The test simply passes a mock to the constructor – because the class was designed for DI.

---

## 6. Take‑Away Checklist

- **Define** interfaces for your services.
- **Implement** the interfaces as Spring components (`@Service`, `@Repository`).
- **Inject** dependencies via constructor (preferred).
- **Configure** any external beans (`DataSource`, `RestTemplate`, etc.) in a `@Configuration` class.
- **Run** your application using `AnnotationConfigApplicationContext` (or Spring Boot, which does this automatically).
- **Test** by injecting mocks directly into the constructors.

That’s it! You now have a fully decoupled, testable Java application powered by Spring’s Dependency Injection. Happy coding!
